{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "title": "Why a second engine",
      "text": "%md\n## The same lakehouse, through Trino\n\nThe previous note read the Delta Lake files with Apache Spark. This one asks **Trino** instead, a\ndistributed SQL engine that queries data where it lies. Zeppelin reaches it through its `jdbc`\ninterpreter, which loads a Java Database Connectivity driver and sends plain SQL over it.\n\n**How the connection is configured.** The `jdbc` interpreter setting in\n`resources/interpreter/interpreter.json` carries four properties:\n\n| property | value |\n| --- | --- |\n| `default.url` | `jdbc:trino://trino:8080` |\n| `default.driver` | `io.trino.jdbc.TrinoDriver` |\n| `default.user` | `zeppelin` |\n| `default.password` | empty |\n\n`default` is a *connection prefix*. One `jdbc` setting can hold several of them - a second prefix\ncalled `warehouse` would be configured as `warehouse.url`, `warehouse.driver`, and selected by writing\n`%jdbc(warehouse)` instead of `%jdbc`. That is how one interpreter serves many databases.\n\nThe driver itself is not bundled with Zeppelin, so it has to be put on the interpreter's classpath.\nZeppelin offers two ways. Each interpreter setting has a **dependencies** list, where a Maven\ncoordinate such as `io.trino:trino-jdbc:476` makes Zeppelin download the jar the first time the\ninterpreter starts. This example takes the other route: the compose stack downloads the jar into\n`/opt/lakehouse/jars`, and the `ZEPPELIN_INTP_CLASSPATH_OVERRIDES` variable puts that folder in front\nof every interpreter's classpath. Nothing is fetched while a reader waits, and the same folder also\ncarries the Hadoop client jars that Zeppelin's jdbc interpreter needs in order to start at all.\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
      "config": {
        "editorSetting": {
          "language": "markdown",
          "editOnDblClick": true,
