    {
      "title": "Interpreter binding and what to try next",
      "text": "%md\n### Interpreter binding\n\nA note does not have to be able to reach every interpreter on the server. **Interpreter binding**, the\ngear icon in the note toolbar, is the per-note list of settings this note may use, and the first entry\nin that list is the note's default - the interpreter a paragraph without a magic falls back to. This\nnote's default is `jdbc`, so removing the `%jdbc` line from a paragraph above changes nothing.\n\nBinding also decides how interpreter processes are shared. In the interpreter list each setting has an\noption for whether one process is shared by every note, or one is started per note, or per user.\nSharing is what makes the temporary views of note 01 visible across its paragraphs; isolating is what\nstops one runaway note from taking down everyone else's session.\n\n### What to try next\n\n1. Run the last paragraph, then open the Trino web interface at <http://localhost:11680> and look at\n   the finished query: it shows the plan Trino chose and how many bytes it read from the object store.\n2. Restart the `spark` interpreter from the interpreter page, then re-run a query paragraph in note 01.\n   The views are gone and the paragraph fails until the Scala paragraph runs again - a concrete\n   demonstration of what interpreter state means.\n",
      "user": "anonymous",
      "config": {
